# ===== VERSION ENDPOINT (must precede SPA catch-all) =====
# Serve the static version artifact instead of the SPA index.html so the
# deploy gate can read the deployed git SHA.
/version /version 200

# ===== VERIFICATION SUMMARY (must precede SPA catch-all) =====
/cta-verification /cta-verification.html 200!

# ===== FORECASTING LANDING (prerendered body; must precede SPA catch-all) =====
# Serve the prerendered flat file at the canonical no-trailing-slash URL so the
# testimonials section is in the raw HTML response. A directory index would make
# Netlify 308-redirect to a trailing slash; a forced flat-file rewrite does not.
/forecasting-landing /forecasting-landing.html 200!
# Bare /forecasting serves the same prerendered body so the testimonials are in
# the raw HTML there too (exact-path match; does not shadow /forecasting-engine
# or the /forecasting-* article URLs).
/forecasting /forecasting-landing.html 200!
/forecasting/ /forecasting-landing.html 200!

# ===== BACKTEST DEMO (prerendered SVG body; must precede SPA catch-all) =====
# Serve the prerendered flat file at the canonical no-trailing-slash URL so the
# SVG chart is in the raw HTML response. A directory index would make Netlify
# 308-redirect to a trailing slash; a forced flat-file rewrite does not. The
# exact-path match does not shadow the /backtest/*.json data artifacts.
/backtest /backtest.html 200!
/backtest/ /backtest.html 200!

# ===== HASHED BUILD ASSETS (must precede SPA catch-all) =====
# Vite emits content-hashed files under /assets/. In a healthy deploy they are
# physically present, so Netlify serves them directly and this rule is never
# consulted. It only fires when a hashed asset is genuinely missing — e.g. a
# browser holding a stale index.html that references an asset from a prior build,
# or a mid-propagation edge. Without this, such a request falls through to the
# SPA catch-all below and returns index.html with Content-Type text/html, which
# Chromium rejects as a module ("MIME type text/html"), so no chart cards render.
# Returning a real 404 keeps a missing asset from masquerading as HTML.
/assets/* /assets/:splat 404

# ===== SPA CATCH-ALL (must be last) =====
/* /index.html 200
